        <div id="forty-percent" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Is a 40% Incline Treadmill Worth It?</h2>
        <p>
          Only if steep walking or hiking is most of what you will do on it.
          The two machines in this guide that go past 40%, the Aviron Summit
          at 42% and the Sunny Versatile at 45%, are dedicated climbing
          machines, and the headline grade comes with conditions that the
          number on the box does not show.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Speed falls as the grade rises.</strong> Aviron caps the
          Summit at 6 mph above 25.5%, and the Sunny tops out at 5 mph at any
          grade. Neither limit matters for steep walking, since few people
          walk faster than 4 mph on a steep slope, but both rule out running
          hill repeats at the top of the range. If you want to run hills as
          well as hike them, a machine that stops at 15 to 20% with a 12 mph
          top speed, such as the THERUN or the Sole F80, is the better fit. For speed above 12 mph, our <a href="/best-fast-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best fast treadmills</a> list machines that reach 14 to 15.6 mph.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It needs a permanent spot and headroom.</strong> The Aviron
          does not fold, weighs 364 lb and measures 67 by 37 by 71 inches, and
          Aviron recommends an 8 ft ceiling to use the full incline range,
          because the front of the deck, and you with it, rise a long way at
          42%. Measure with your tallest user in mind before you order.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>It costs roughly three times as much as 20%.</strong> On
          the days we checked, the Sunny was $2,199.99 and the Aviron $2,849
          with its smaller screen, against $799.99 for the 20% THERUN and
          $1,499.99 for the 15% Sole F80. That premium buys a stronger incline
          drive and frame, and for a hiker training for steep mountain terrain
          it is money well spent. For someone who will mostly walk at 5 to 15%,
          it buys grade they will rarely use.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Forty percent is steeper than it sounds, in a useful way.</strong>
          Grade is rise over run, so 45% is about 24 degrees, and even 15 to
          20% at walking pace is hard work. Our explainer on
          <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill incline percent vs degrees</a>
          converts the figures, and our comparison of an
          <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainer vs a treadmill</a>
          sets out who the dedicated machines suit. If you want incline at a
          desk rather than in a home gym, a pad tops out far lower; see the
          <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads with incline</a>.
        </p>
        </div>

        <div id="high-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">What Counts as a High-Incline Treadmill?</h2>
        <p>
          Most home treadmills stop at 15%, so in practice "high incline"
          means anything that goes past that, and the machines that do fall
          into four quite different groups. The grade is only part of the
          difference: how the incline is driven, how fast you can go at the
          top of it and what the machine costs change just as much from one
          group to the next.
        </p>
